    "justification": "The test set has 6,000-6,000 examples per class (imbalance ratio 1.00). That is effectively balanced, so accuracy is not inflated by a majority class and is the right headline metric. It is also what every published result on this benchmark reports, which is what makes comparison possible. Macro-F1 is reported alongside it as a safeguard: because it weights every class equally, a model that quietly abandoned one class would show up there while accuracy stayed respectable. Micro-F1 is identical to accuracy in single-label classification, so it adds nothing here. AUC is not used because this is a 10-class decision reported at a fixed operating point, not a ranking problem with a tunable threshold."
